The French press is about as simple and quick as coffee gets. You put coffee and hot water in, wait, press and pour. It makes a full-bodied, rich cup, and it's a good match for darker roasts.

This is part two of [Brew Methods](/coffee/guides/coffee-brew-methods/).

## How it works

A French press is an immersion brewer. The grounds soak in the water for the whole brew, then a metal mesh screen pushes them to the bottom. Because the coffee steeps instead of having water run through it, it's very forgiving. You don't have to pour carefully, and small changes in grind matter less than they do for pour-over.

The metal screen is what gives it its character. Unlike paper, it lets the coffee oils and some very fine particles through. That's where the heavy body comes from, and it's also where the sludge comes from.

## Why darker roasts work well

The heavy body and rounded flavor of a French press go nicely with medium-dark and dark roasts: chocolate, nuts, caramel and toasty flavors. Light roasts work too, but the bright, delicate notes can get a little lost under all that body. If you love light roasts, [pour-over](/coffee/guides/pour-over-coffee/) shows them off better.

## Avoiding sludge

The usual way to brew a French press is to steep for 4 minutes, press all the way down and pour. It works, but you end up with a cloudy cup and a gritty last sip. A little patience fixes most of that.

<CoffeeBrewRecipe {...recipe} />

It takes a few more minutes, but most of that is waiting, not work. And the coffee in the press stays hot longer than you'd think.

## Tips

- **Grind for it.** Very fine grounds slip through the screen. A good [burr grinder](/coffee/reviews/hand-coffee-grinders/) makes fewer fines than a blade grinder.
- **Don't leave it sitting.** Coffee left on the grounds keeps brewing and gets bitter. Pour it all out into a cup or a thermal carafe.
- **Pressing is hard?** The grind is too fine. Go coarser next time.
- **Clean the screen.** Old coffee oils build up in the mesh and go stale. Take the plunger apart and wash it every so often.
